Independent reproduction of prior-audit finding P1-1 (JIT degrades after 1st call)
Date: 2026-11-20 | Binary: rust/target/release/un.exe (built from current WIP source)

Repro programs (created under docs/audit/, no user files touched):
  jit_repro_one.un : fn drive(n) with n=1,000,000, called ONCE
  jit_repro_two.un : same function, called TWICE

Wall-clock ms (3 reps default JIT, 2 reps UV_JIT=0):
  one_call defaultJIT ms: 28.4, 27.2, 26.1
  two_call defaultJIT ms: 1268.3, 1273.9, 1254.1
  one_call UV_JIT=0 ms: 1271.5, 1255.3
  two_call UV_JIT=0 ms: 2451.6, 2460.2

Interpretation:
  - 1st call default JIT ~27ms => native JIT active.
  - 2nd call default JIT adds ~1238ms => ~= one full interpreter pass (1263ms).
  - With UV_JIT=0 both calls are interpreter (~1263ms each).
  => Under default configuration the JIT only accelerates the FIRST invocation of
     an argument-taking proto; every later invocation silently falls back.

Static root cause (independently read from source):
  - jit_cache.rs:70  JIT_FNS is a single shared map for bytecode JIT and TIR entries.
  - jit_cache.rs:140 get_jit_entry and :147 get_tir_entry both read that SAME map.
  - jit_bc.rs:608-614 register_jit_entry(...) stores bytecode entries with
    arg_kinds: Vec::new().
  - jit_fast.rs:72 try_call_jit consults get_tir_entry FIRST and, on hit, returns
    try_call_tir_entry(...) immediately (:73-75), never reaching bytecode path (:76).
  - jit_fast.rs:20 try_call_tir_entry rejects when
    entry.arg_kinds.len() != args.values.len()  (0 vs N), returning None => interpreter.
  => 1st call: no entry yet -> bytecode JIT registered & used.
     2nd call: bytecode entry is misread as a TIR entry -> arity/kind mismatch -> None.
